#!/usr/local/bin/python3.13 -I
"""watch: cheap, polite web watches. Cron checks them for free; they notify only on a change or a threshold.

  watch add price SYMBOL above|below X [--currency chf]
        crypto: BTC, ETH, SOL… or cg:<coingecko-id> (CoinGecko public API, in CHF by default);
        FX: EUR/CHF (Frankfurter, ECB rates); anything else is a stock/ETF via Yahoo chart v8, which its
        robots.txt currently refuses (see "Refusals")
  watch add feed URL [--match RE] [--exclude RE]          RSS/Atom: new items, deduped by guid/id
  watch add release OWNER/REPO [--match RE] [--exclude RE]  GitHub releases (releases.atom)
  watch add page URL [--select CSS | --region-regex RE] [--contains T | --gone T | --changed]
        --select: CSS-ish (tag, .class, #id, [attr=val], descendant " " and child ">", "a, b");
        --region-regex runs on the visible text (group 1 if any). Default trigger: --changed.
  watch add json URL --path a.b.0.c --op lt|gt|eq|changed [--value V]
  watch add offer URL --below X [--match "words"]
        shop prices: JSON-LD Product/ItemList offers on any allowed site, plus Alternate.ch and MediaMarkt.ch
        listings; fires once per product that drops below X (re-arms after a 2 % rise). --match keeps products whose
        name has all the words ("22TB" = "22 TB"). `deals watch` wraps this (Alternate search, probe first).
     options for every add:
        --owner | --me      who's told: the owner via `tg` ("🔔 …"), or me via an ~/inbox note (default --me)
        --every 30m|2h|1d   check interval (min 15m; default: price 30m, release/offer 6h, others 1h)
        --until YYYY-MM-DD  last day; the watch is removed after it
        --note "why"        context, shown in `list` and in my inbox notes
        --name LABEL        label used in messages
        --once              remove the watch after its first notification
  watch list                  watches with status
  watch rm N                  remove watch N (alias: remove)
  watch enable N              re-enable a watch that was disabled after 5 failures
  watch check N               fetch now and show what it sees (no notification, nothing saved)
  watch run [--force]         (cron */15) check due watches; --force ignores the per-watch interval

Triggers: owner and my watches alike notify once, then disarm. Price watches re-arm after a 2 % move back
(hysteresis), --contains/--gone/lt/gt/eq re-arm once the condition is false again, feeds/releases notify
each new item once, --changed notifies each change once. The first check of a feed/release/--changed watch
only records a baseline.
Politeness: identifies itself with a polite User-Agent, at least 15 min between requests to the same host
(official APIs: at most 10 requests per run instead), ETag/If-Modified-Since, exponential backoff on errors
(Retry-After honoured); after 5 consecutive failures the watch is disabled and I get an ~/inbox note.
Refusals: hosts whose terms forbid automated access (ra.co: RA terms 4.4(f); LinkedIn; Meta), hosts whose bot
protection blocks honest automated clients (Galaxus/Digitec/Brack, Interdiscount/Microspot, Ricardo, Toppreise; never
bypassed; re-test with `deals sources --check`), and any URL its host's robots.txt disallows
(checked once per host, cached 7 days). Official documented public APIs
(CoinGecko, Frankfurter, api.github.com) are used under their API terms; their robots.txt targets crawlers.
State: ~/.local/state/watch/ (state.json, watch.log). Tests: WATCH_STATE_DIR, WATCH_INBOX, WATCH_TG (sender),
WATCH_NOW (epoch or "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM"), WATCH_HOST_GAP (seconds), WATCH_YAHOO_BASE, WATCH_COINGECKO_BASE,
WATCH_FRANKFURTER_BASE, WATCH_GITHUB_BASE, WATCH_TIMEOUT.
A procps-style call (`watch -n 2 cmd`, `watch cmd`) is handed to /usr/bin/watch.
"""
